-------------------------------------------------------------------

The $@ is a Make automatic variable that represents the target of the rule.
In this case:

When Make builds obj/ft_strlen.o, $@ expands to obj/ft_strlen.o
When Make builds obj/ft_bzero.o, $@ expands to obj/ft_bzero.o
And so on...

So the command:

$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c $< -o $@

Expands to something like:

cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -c ft_strlen.c -o obj/ft_strlen.o

Common Make automatic variables:
$@ - The target filename
$< - The first prerequisite (in your case, the .c file)
$^ - All prerequisites
$? - All prerequisites newer than the target

-------------------------------------------------------------------

.h (Header file)
Contains declarations that tell the compiler what exists without implementing it:

Function prototypes (signatures without the actual code)
Struct, union, and enum definitions
Macro definitions (#define)
Type definitions (typedef)
Global variable declarations (extern)
Included by other files using #include
- Purpose: Allows multiple .c files to know about shared functions/types without duplicating code.


.o (Object file)
Compiled machine code that's not yet a complete program:

Generated by the compiler from a single .c file
Contains machine instructions specific to your CPU architecture
Has unresolved references (calls to functions defined elsewhere)
Not executable on its own
Binary format (not human-readable)
Waiting to be linked with other .o files
- Purpose: Intermediate step between source code and executable. Allows separate compilation - you only recompile changed files.


.a (Archive/Static library)
A collection of .o files packaged into one file:

Created using the ar (archiver) utility
Acts as a library of reusable compiled code
Gets linked directly into your executable at compile time
The linker extracts only the .o files you actually use
Makes your final executable larger (code is copied in)
Platform-specific (Linux/Unix convention; Windows uses .lib)
- Purpose: Distribute compiled code without source. Users link against it to use your functions without recompiling them.